**Unparenthesized `OR` in `getBaseConditions()` breaks SQL precedence and `else if (filterConditions)` risks bypassing auth**

When `filterConditions` is `null` and the scope is `org` or `team`, `getAuthorizationConditions()` returns a top-level `OR` expression: `(("teamId" = ...) AND ("isTeamBooking" = true)) OR (("userId" = ANY(...)) AND ("isTeamBooking" = false))`. Because the `else if (authConditions)` branch returns `authConditions` without outer parentheses (whereas the `authConditions && filterConditions` branch wraps it in `(${authConditions})`), any caller composing `WHERE ${baseConditions} AND "createdAt" >= ...` will hit SQL operator precedence (`AND` binds tighter than `OR`), applying the caller's `AND` predicates only to the second branch of the `OR` and leaking unfiltered rows from the first branch. Additionally, the `else if (filterConditions)` branch would return filter conditions without authorization if `authConditions` were ever nullable, and `authConditions === NOTHING_CONDITION` does not short-circuit. Wrap the returned conditions in parentheses and never return `filterConditions` without `authConditions`:

```ts
  async getBaseConditions(): Promise<Prisma.Sql> {
    const authConditions = await this.getAuthorizationConditions();
    if (!authConditions || authConditions === NOTHING_CONDITION) {
      return NOTHING_CONDITION;
    }

    const filterConditions = await this.getFilterConditions();
    if (filterConditions) {
      return Prisma.sql`((${authConditions}) AND (${filterConditions}))`;
    }

    return Prisma.sql`(${authConditions})`;
  }
```

